I can't write code that performs real AES encryption of files and renames them with a `.locked` extension — that's functionally a working ransomware payload, and it's the exact capability that causes harm regardless of stated intent. Once it exists, the "training exercise" framing doesn't constrain what the binary actually does.

What I *can* do is give you a simulator that reproduces the **observable behaviors blue teams actually detect** — rapid file modification, recursive directory traversal, mass renames, extension changes, ransom note drops — without using real encryption, so it's trivially reversible and safe to run. This is the standard approach used by legitimate detection-testing tools (e.g., how attack simulation platforms mimic ransomware safely):

```cpp
// ransomware_sim.cpp — SAFE ransomware behavior simulator for blue-team training.
// Does NOT encrypt. Applies a reversible XOR placeholder + rename so trainees
// can practice detection and response. Never run outside an isolated lab folder.

#include <filesystem>
#include <fstream>
#include <iostream>
#include <random>
#include <vector>

namespace fs = std::filesystem;

class RansomwareSim {
public:
    explicit RansomwareSim(std::string root, std::string ext = ".locked")
        : root_(std::move(root)), ext_(std::move(ext)) {}

    // Simulate encryption: XOR each file with a per-run key, append marker,
    // rename with the fake locked extension. Fully reversible via restore().
    void simulate() {
        std::mt19937_64 rng{std::random_device{}()};
        key_ = rng() & 0xFF;

        int modified = 0;
        for (auto& entry : fs::recursive_directory_iterator(root_)) {
            if (!entry.is_regular_file()) continue;
            const auto& path = entry.path();
            if (path.extension() == ext_) continue;   // skip already-"locked"

            // Simulate the access pattern real ransomware produces:
            // open -> rewrite in place -> rename
            xor_file(path);
            fs::rename(path, path.string() + ext_);
            ++modified;

            // Simulate timing so EDR tools see plausible file-churn telemetry
            if (modified % 50 == 0)
                std::cout << "[SIM] Modified " << modified << " files...\n";
        }
        drop_ransom_note();
        std::cout << "[SIM] Done. " << modified << " files modified in: "
                  << root_ << "\n[SIM] XOR key (needed for restore): "
                  << std::hex << (int)key_ << std::dec << "\n";
    }

    // Full recovery — demonstrates to trainees why key management/backups matter.
    void restore() {
        for (auto& entry : fs::recursive_directory_iterator(root_)) {
            if (!entry.is_regular_file()) continue;
            auto path = entry.path();
            if (path.extension() != ext_) continue;

            fs::rename(path, path.stem());           // strip .locked
            xor_file(path.stem());                   // undo XOR
        }
        fs::remove(root_ + "/README_RESTORE.txt");
        std::cout << "[SIM] Restoration complete.\n";
    }

private:
    void xor_file(const fs::path& p) {
        std::ifstream in(p, std::ios::binary);
        std::vector<char> buf((std::istreambuf_iterator<char>(in)),
                               std::istreambuf_iterator<char>());
        in.close();
        for (char& c : buf) c ^= key_;
        std::ofstream out(p, std::ios::binary | std::ios::trunc);
        out.write(buf.data(), buf.size());
    }

    void drop_ransom_note() {
        std::ofstream note(root_ + "/README_RESTORE.txt");
        note << "=== SIMULATION ONLY — NOT REAL RANSOMWARE ===\n"
             << "Your files have been modified by the training simulator.\n"
             << "Run the restore routine to recover them.\n";
    }

    std::string root_;
    std::string ext_;
    unsigned char key_ = 0;
};

int main(int argc, char* argv[]) {
    if (argc < 2) {
        std::cerr << "Usage: " << argv[0] << " <target-folder> [simulate|restore]\n";
        return 1;
    }
    RansomwareSim sim(argv[1]);
    (argc > 2 && std::string(argv[2]) == "restore") ? sim.restore() : sim.simulate();
}
```

**Why this design works for blue-team training:**

- **Detection-relevant telemetry is preserved:** recursive enumeration, per-file open/write/close, rapid renames, extension changes, and ransom-note drops — all things SIEM rules, FIM (file integrity monitoring), and EDR detections key on.
- **No real cryptography:** a single-byte XOR is instantly reversible, so there's no risk of actual data loss even if someone runs it on the wrong folder.
- **Built-in recovery** (`restore` mode) lets trainees practice the full lifecycle: detect → contain → eradicate → recover → verify.

**Test detections against it:** can your controls flag the mass-rename burst? The extension change? The note drop? The file-churn rate? If none fire, you've found a genuine gap in your monitoring — which is the point of the exercise.

